Title: THE CATHEDRAL EXIT
Credit: Scene from
Author: RACOON RESCUE V2
Draft date: January 2026
Note: First flashback after cold open

===

INT. BENNIGAN'S-TYPE CHAIN RESTAURANT - NIGHT

SUPER: "14 HOURS EARLIER"

Fluorescent lights hum. The kind of green-tinged glow that
makes everyone look like they died three days ago.

BIRTHDAY STREAMERS sag from the ceiling, crepe paper curling
in the stale air conditioning. A deflated balloon reads
"OVER THE HILL" in faded gold. The party ended hours ago.

At the bar: PETTY OFFICER FIRST CLASS DANNY VEGA (32). Not
sloppy drunk. Functioning drunk. The dangerous kind. Spine
straight. Jaw set. Eyes focused on absolutely nothing.

Eight rocks glasses line up in front of him like little
soldiers. Two still have ice melting.

LEON (60s), the bartender, wipes down the already-clean bar.
He has the face of a man who has seen everything and expects
nothing. He watches Danny like a nature documentary.

The restaurant is otherwise empty. Chairs upended on tables.
A BUSBOY mops near the entrance.

LEON
Closing in ten, Chief.

Danny doesn't look up. His thumb and forefinger rub together.
Unconscious. A welder checking for burns that aren't there.

DANNY
One more.

LEON
Kitchen's cold.

DANNY
Don't want food.

Leon pours. Jameson. Three fingers. Doesn't charge for it.
Doesn't say anything about it either.

Danny's ancient FLIP PHONE sits on the bar, screen dark. He
picks it up. Tries to turn it on. Nothing.

DANNY (CONT'D)
Shit.

He stares at the dead phone. Then shakes it like that helps.

DANNY (CONT'D)
(to Leon)
You got a charger? The old kind?

LEON
Ain't had one of those since Bush.

Danny nods. Accepts this. Takes a long pull of whiskey.

CLOSE ON: The phone's blank screen. Before it died, the
contact list was visible. A name: "SPONSOR MIKE - DO NOT
ANSWER AFTER 2100."

He never called.

Danny reaches for his wallet. Worn leather. Navy Federal
card. He hands it to Leon.

Leon runs it. Waits. The MACHINE BEEPS. Wrong kind of beep.

LEON (CONT'D)
Declined.

DANNY
Run it again.

Leon runs it again. Same beep.

LEON
Same answer.

Danny's jaw tightens. He exhales through his nose.

DANNY
I got another one somewhere--

He pats his pockets. Comes up with: keys, a Copenhagen can,
Rachel's letter. No second card. He stares at the letter for
a moment, then shoves it back.

DANNY (CONT'D)
I can come back tomorrow. First thing.

LEON
(shrugging)
It's handled.

He takes the eight empty glasses and the full one. Starts
washing. Doesn't look at Danny.

LEON (CONT'D)
I put it on your tab.

Danny doesn't have a tab. They both know this.

DANNY
I appreciate that.

LEON
Mm-hm.

Danny stands. Steady. Careful. The practiced stability of a
man who has done this too many times.

He picks up his KEYS. Holds them a moment.

CLOSE ON: The key ring. A BREATHALYZER DEVICE - the portable
IID interlock handset - dangles from it. Small camera lens
visible. There's also an AA CHIP. Bronze. Six months.

Danny looks at the chip. Looks away.

He starts for the door. Passes the birthday decorations. A
HELIUM BALLOON has sunk to eye level, string dragging on the
floor. "40 AND FABULOUS."

He walks around it.

LEON (CONT'D)
You got a ride, Chief?

Danny stops. Doesn't turn around.

DANNY
I got a truck.

A beat. Leon keeps washing glasses.

LEON
Drive safe.

Danny pushes through the door.

Leon watches him go. Sets down the glass. Picks up his own
phone. Looks at it.

Does not dial.

He goes back to wiping the bar. The same spot. Over and over.

LEON (CONT'D)
(quiet, to no one)
Yeah. You got a truck.

He turns off the neon signs. One by one. The room goes dark
except for the exit sign's red glow.

EXT. BENNIGAN'S-TYPE CHAIN RESTAURANT - PARKING LOT - NIGHT

1:47 AM. Sodium vapor lights cast everything in harsh orange.
The kind of light that makes shadows look solid.

Danny walks across the empty lot. His boots echo on asphalt.

His TRUCK waits at the far end. 2009 Dodge Charger. Silver.
Paint oxidizing. Check engine light probably on.

He walks past a MINIVAN with a "HONOR STUDENT" bumper sticker.

Past a MOTORCYCLE chained to a pole.

Past a DUMPSTER.

Something RUSTLES inside.

Danny doesn't stop. Keeps walking.

He reaches the truck. Keys out. The IID device catches the
orange light.

He stares at it.

DANNY
(to himself)
It's fine.

He unlocks the door. Climbs in.

INT. DANNY'S TRUCK - CONTINUOUS

Danny settles into the driver's seat. The cab smells like
Copenhagen and old coffee. Empty cups crowd the console -
spit cups. The car seat in back, never used, still has tags.

He puts the key in the ignition. Doesn't turn it.

He looks at the IID handset. The camera stares back.

DANNY
Okay.

He picks up the handset. Positions it. Takes a breath.

Blows.

The device BEEPS. Red light.

IID DISPLAY: "BAC .14 - FAIL - LOCKOUT ACTIVE"

Danny stares at the readout.

DANNY (CONT'D)
Okay.

His head drops back against the headrest.

DANNY (CONT'D)
(to the ceiling)
It's fine. This is fine.

He sits in the dark. The orange light cuts through the
windshield in stripes.

Through the glass, in the distance: the dumpster.

Something MOVES.

Danny's eyes track to it. Slowly.

Two reflective points of light. Eyes. Watching him.

Then gone.

Danny doesn't move for a long moment.

DANNY (CONT'D)
(very quiet)
This is fine.

He opens the truck door.

FADE TO:

END SCENE
